In France, voting is traditionally an experiment of low technicality: voters isolate themselves in a cabin, put in an envelope a sheet of pre-printed paper indicating the candidate of their choice.
After the agents have verified his identity, the elector throws the envelope into the urn and signs his voter card.
The French Electoral Code regulates operations fairly strictly.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent so that voters and observers can find that no envelope is present at the beginning of the vote and that no envelopes are added, except those of duly counted and authorized voters.
