Below there are medium-sized felines that eat equally medium-sized prey, ranging from rabbits to antelopes and other deer.
In addition, there are many small cats (including free-ranging pet cats) that eat small prey, far more numerous, such as insects, rodents, lizards and birds.
The secret of their success is the concept of niche, a special task devolved to every feline, preventing it from competing with others.
Lions are the most sociable cats, living in large groups called packs.
The packs are composed of one to three related adult males, as well as about thirty females and lion cubs.
Women are usually very close to each other, their family being composed of a large number of sisters and daughters.
Lion troops act as packs of wolves or dogs, animals surprisingly similar to lions (but not the other big felines) in their behavior, and also very mortal for their prey.
Balanced athlete, the tiger can climb (but sometimes difficult), swim, jump from great distances and tow with five times the strength of a powerful human.
The tiger is part of the same family (genus Panthera) as lions, leopards and jaguars. These four felines are the only ones capable of roaring.
The roar of the tiger does not resemble the lion’s ample roar, but rather to a phrase whose words would be screaming and rumbling.
Ocelots are fond of small animals. They catch monkeys, snakes, rodents and birds if they can. Almost all the animals that the ocelot hunts are much smaller than it.
According to the scientists, ocelots track down and find the animals to eat (prey) by smell, sniffing their journey on the ground.
They see very well in the dark thanks to their night vision, and also move very stealthily. Ocelots track down their prey by blending into their environment before throwing themselves at them.
When a small group of living beings (a small population) is separated from the main population from which it comes (e.g., if it moves over a mountain range or river, or if it moves to a new island so that it cannot easily go back), it often finds itself in an environment different from the one in which it was before.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population will need new characteristics or adaptations to be a powerful competitor compared to what they needed before.
The original population has not changed at all, it still needs the same adaptations.
Over time, as the new population adapts to its new environment, it begins to look less and less like the other population.
Finally, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will appear so different that they can no longer be considered belonging to the same species.
We call this process “speciation”, which simply means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
