On August 15, 1940, the Allies landed in the south of France, an invasion called Operation Dragoon.
In just two weeks, the Americans and the Forces of Free France had liberated the south of France and were heading towards Germany.
A civilization is a singular culture shared by a large group of people who live and work cooperatively, a society.
The term “civilization” is derived from the Latin word “civilis”, which means civil and is related to the Latin words “civis”, which means citizen, and “civitas”, which means great city or city-state and which also indicates, in some way, the size of the society to which it relates.
Cities-states are the precursors of nations. A civilizational culture involves the transmission of knowledge over several generations, a sustainable cultural imprint and equitable dissemination.
Minority cultures often disappear without leaving relevant historical traces and are not recognized as true civilizations.
During the Revolutionary War, the thirteen states first formed a weak central government — the Congress being its only component — under the articles of Confederation.
The Congress did not have the power to impose taxes and, since there was no national executive or judiciary, it relied on the state authorities, often unco-operative, to enforce all its actions.
Nor did it have the power to derogate from tax laws or customs duties between states.
The articles required the unanimous consent of all States before they could be amended, and States took the Central Government so lightly that their representatives were often absent.
