Japan has nearly 7 000 islands (the largest being Honshu), making it the 7th largest island in the world!
Due to its group of islands, Japan is often geographically referred to as an "Archipelago".
The first information about Taiwan dates back to the 15th century, while European sailors passing by mentioned it under the name Ilha Formosa, or “beautiful island”.
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in south-western Taiwan, introducing a processing of indigenous cereal production practices and employing Chinese workers to work on its rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, the forces of the Qing Dynasty (1644-1912) took control of Taiwan's western and northern coastal areas and declared it the province of the Qing Empire in 1885.
In 1895, after its defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Shimonoseki Treaty, by which it ceded sovereignty from Taiwan to Japan, which then ruled the island until 1945.
