Muhammad was deeply interested in questions that went beyond the framework of this worldly life. He used to frequent a cave that became famous under the name “Hira’” on the mountain of “Noor” (light) for contemplation.
The cave itself, which survived at the time, gives a very vivid picture of Muhammad’s spiritual inclinations.
Located at the top of one of the mountains north of Mecca, the cave is totally isolated from the rest of the world.
In reality, it's not easy to find at all, even if we know it exists, and when we've entered the cave, we're completely isolated.
Above, one cannot contemplate anything other than the clear and beautiful sky and the many surrounding mountains. From the inside of the cave, you can only see or hear very few things from this universe.
The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only one of the seven wonders that still exists today.
Constructed by the Egyptians during the third millennium BC, the Great Pyramid is one of the many giant structures erected in honor of the deceased pharaohs.
The Giza Plateau, or "Giza Necropolis", in the Valley of the Dead in Egypt, gathers several pyramids (the largest pyramid being the most imposing), several small tombs, many temples and the great Sphinx.
The Great Pyramid was created to honor Pharaoh Kheops, and many small pyramids, tombs and temples were built to honor his wives as well as members of his family.
