The Central Middle Ages was preceded by the High Middle Ages and followed by the Early Middle Ages, which by convention ended around 1500.
Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a wide range of ideas in practice, ranging from the technological thrust or technological imperative to a strict sense that the fate of humanity is dictated by an underlying logic related to scientific laws and their manifestation in technologies.
Most interpretations of technological determinism agree on two general ideas: the technological development itself unfolds according to a process far beyond cultural and political influences, and technology has an impact on societies conditioned intrinsically rather than socially.
For example, it could be said that the automobile necessarily leads to the development of roads.
However, a national road network is not economically viable for a handful of cars, which is why new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of owning a car.
Massive possession of cars also leads to a greater impact of accidents on the roads, which leads to the invention of new techniques in the field of health care to care for bodily harm.
Romanticism presented an important element of cultural determinism, drawn from writers such as Goethe, Fichte and Schlegel.
According to the theories of Romanticism, geography would have shaped individuals and, over time, customs and a culture related to this geography would have appeared. These, in harmony with the places occupied by society, would be preferable to the laws arbitrarily imposed.
In the same way that Paris is known as the fashion capital of the contemporary world, Constantinople was regarded as that of feudal Europe.
Its reputation as an epicenter of luxury began around the year 400, and lasted up to about 1100.
Its status declined during the twelfth century, mainly due to the fact that the Crusaders took care of presents such as silk and spices, with which the Byzantine markets could hardly compete.
It was at this time that the title of fashion capital was transferred from Constantinople to Paris.
The Gothic style peaked between the 10th and 14th centuries.
At first, the dress was heavily influenced by Byzantine culture in the east.
However, due to the slower means of communication, Western modes could lag behind by 25 to 30 years.
During the late Middle Ages, Western Europe began to develop its own style. One of the greatest advances of the time, following the crusades, was the use of buttons for the closure of clothes.
Subsistenctive farming is an agriculture practiced to produce enough food to meet only the needs of the farmer and his family.
Subsistence farming is a simple, often organic system, using preserved seeds originating from the ecoregion, combined with crop rotation or other relatively simple techniques to maximize yield.
Historically, most farmers engaged in subsistence farming and this is still the case in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together individuals who feel neglected by societal norms and share the same ideas which allows them to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures can be distinguished by age, ethnicity, class, location and/or gender of members.
Criteria that determine a subculture as distinct can be linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical or a combination of factors.
The members of a subculture often indicate their belonging through a distinctive and symbolic use of style, which encompasses fashions, mannerisms and slang.
One of the most common methods used to illustrate the importance of socialization is to draw inspiration from the few unfortunate cases of children who, through negligence, misfortune or voluntary abuse, have not been socialized by adults during their childhood.
These children are considered "ferocious" or savage. Some wild children have been locked up by people (usually their own parents); in some cases, the abandonment of this child was due to the rejection by parents of a serious intellectual or physical disability suffered by the child.
Savage children may have experienced severe abuse or trauma before being abandoned or fleeing.
Others would have been raised by animals; some would have lived alone in nature.
When they are entirely raised by non-human species, wild children display behaviors (within their physical dispositions) almost identical to those of animals that raised them, such as fear or indifference to humans.
While project pedagogy should make learning easier and more interesting, support goes further.
Support is not a method of learning, but rather a way to help learners who are discovering new education such as using a new curriculum or starting a new project.
The supports can be both virtual and real. In other words, a teacher is a form of support, but the little trombone man from Microsoft Office is one too.
Virtual scaffolds are integrated into the software and are intended to query, guide and explain procedures that could have been too difficult to manage for the student alone.
Children are placed in foster families for a wide variety of reasons ranging from negligence to abuse to extortion.
No child should ever have to grow up in an environment that is not stimulating, caring and educational, but that is the case.
We perceive the foster care system as a safety zone for these children.
Our foster care system is supposed to provide safe homes, loving caregivers, stable education and reliable health care.
Foster care is supposed to provide any necessities that were lacking in the home from which they were previously removed.
The Internet combines elements of mass communication and interpersonal communication.
The distinct features of the Internet lead to additional dimensions in terms of approaching uses and gratifications.
For example, "learning" and "socialization" are considered to be major factors that promote Internet use (James et al. 1995).
“Personal Involvement” and “continuing Relationships” have also been identified as new motivational aspects by Eighmey and McCord (1998) when they have studied the public’s reactions to websites.
The use of video recording led to great discoveries in the interpretation of micro-expressions, i.e. facial expressions for a few milliseconds.
In particular, it is claimed that one can detect whether a person lies by correctly interpreting microexpressions.
