It even suggests that such ability to interpret human behavior may be found in animals such as domestic dogs.
Research of the twentieth century has shown that there are two reservoirs of genetic variation: hidden and expressed.
The mutation adds a new genetic variation, and the selection removes it from the reserve of the expressed variations.
Segregation and recombination alternate variations between the two basins with each generation.
In savannah, it’s difficult for a primate with a digestive system similar to that of humans to satisfy its amino acid needs from available plant resources.
In addition, a lack of reaction has serious consequences: growth disorders, malnutrition and ultimately death.
The most easily accessible plant resources would be the proteins contained in leaves and legumes, but they are difficult to digest for primates like us unless cooked.
In contrast, food of animal origin (ants, termites, eggs) is easy to digest while providing a large amount of protein containing all vital amino acids.
All things considered, we shouldn’t be surprised if our own ancestors solved their “protein problem” somewhat in the same way the chimpanzees of the savannah do today.
Interruption of sleep is the process of intentional awakening during your period of sleep and drowsiness shortly thereafter (10 to 60 minutes).
This can be done easily by using a relatively silent awakening, in order to make you regain consciousness without waking up completely.
If you find yourself resetting the clock during your sleep, it can be placed on the other side of the room, forcing you out of bed to turn it off.
Other biorhythm-based possibilities include drinking plenty of fluids before falling asleep (including water or tea, known to be diuretic), which forces you to wake up to urinate.
A person’s degree of inner peace opposes the degree of tension in his body and mind.
The lower the tension, the more positive the present life force. Each person has the opportunity to find absolute peace and satisfaction.
Everyone can achieve enlightenment. The only thing that stands in the way of this goal is our own tension and negativity.
Tibetan Buddhism is based on Buddha’s teachings, but has been enriched by the path of Mahayana love and by many techniques of Indian yoga.
In principle, Tibetan Buddhism is very simple. It consists of Kundalini Yoga, meditation and the path of universal love.
With Kundalini Yoga, Kundalini energy (enlightening energy) is awakened by yoga postures, breathing exercises, mantras and visualizations.
The center of Tibetan meditation is deity yoga. It is thanks to the visualization of different deities that energy channels are cleansed, chakras activated, and awakening consciousness created.
Germany was a common enemy during the Second World War, which led to cooperation between the USSR and the United States. At the end of the war, deep conflicts between their different systems, methods and cultures put an end to the understanding between these two countries.
Two years after the end of the war, the former allies became enemies and the Cold War began.
It would last for the next 40 years and would see real clashes, through surrogacy armies, on battlefields both in Africa, Asia, Afghanistan, Cuba and many other places.
On September 17, 1939, the Polish defense was already broken, and the only hope was to retreat and reorganize along the Romanian bridgehead.
But these plans have become obsolete almost overnight, when more than 800 000 soldiers from the Red Army of the Soviet Union arrived and created the Belarusian and Ukrainian front after invading the eastern regions of Poland, thus violating the Riga Peace Treaty, the Soviet-Polish non-aggression pact, as well as other international treaties, whether bilateral or multilateral.
Vessel use for the transportation of goods is by far the most efficient way to move large quantities of people and goods across the oceans.
Marine work has traditionally been to ensure that your country retains the ability to move your people and belongings, while interfering with your enemy’s ability to move its people and property.
One of the most remarkable recent examples of this situation was the North Atlantic campaign during the Second World War. The Americans were trying to cross the Atlantic Ocean to soldiers and equipment to help Britain.
At the same time, the German navy, using mainly U-boats, was trying to stop this traffic.
If the Allies had failed, Germany probably could have conquered Britain as it did for the rest of Europe.
It seems that the goats were domesticated for the first time about 10 000 years ago in the Zagros Mountains in Iran.
Ancient cultures and tribes have begun to keep them close to them for easy access to milk, hair, meat and skins.
Domestic goats were usually kept in herds that roamed the hills or other grazing spaces, often cared for by goathers who were mostly children or teenagers, similar to the better-known end of shepherds. These pastoral methods are still ongoing today.
The wagonways were built in England as early as the 16th century.
Although the wagon tracks were simply made up of parallel wooden planks, they allowed the horses pulling them to reach higher speeds and to pull larger loads than on the slightly rougher roads of the day.
The braces were introduced early enough to keep the tracks in place; however, it was gradually realized that the tracks would be more effective if they were equipped with an iron strip on the top.
This has become a common practice, but iron has caused more wear on the wooden wheels of wagons.
Finally, the wooden wheels were replaced by iron wheels. In 1767, the first iron rails appeared.
The very first means of locomotion was walking: humans began to walk two million years ago with the arrival of Homo Erectus (“right man”).
Their predecessors, the Australopitheques, did not walk as usual.
Bipedal specializations can be found in Australopithecus fossils from 4.2 to 3.9 million years ago, although the Sahelanthropus was able to walk on two legs already seven million years ago.
We can start by living in better harmony with the environment, we can join the environmental movement, and we can even campaign in order to alleviate the suffering to come.
In many cases, this is symptomatic treatment. However, if we don’t just want a temporary solution, we need to find the root of the problems and disable them.
It is clear that the world has changed a lot thanks to the scientific and technological advances made by human beings, and that the problems have become more important because of humanity's overpopulation and extravagant way of life.
Following its passage by Congress on July 4, a handwritten project signed by Congress President John Hancock and Secretary Charles Thomson was then sent a few streets away to John Dunlap's printing press.
During the night, between 150 and 200 copies were made, now known as “Dunlap Broadsides”.
The first public reading of the document was made by John Nixon in the courtyard of Independence Hall on July 8.
One of them was sent to George Washington on 6 July, which made him read to his troops in New York on July 9. A copy arrived in London on August 10.
The 25 Dunlap sheets that are still known are the oldest copies of the document. The original handwritten copy did not survive.
Many paleontologists today believe that a group of dinosaurs have survived and is still alive. They are called birds.
Few people consider them to be dinosaurs because they have wings and can fly.
But there’s a lot about birds that still look like a dinosaur.
They have scale-covered paws with claws, lay eggs and walk on their twicks like T-Rex.
Virtually all computers used today are based on the manipulation of information that is encoded in the form of binary numbers.
A binary number can have only one of two values: 0 or 1. These numbers are called binary digits, or bits to use computer jargon.
Internal poisoning may not be visible immediately. Symptoms, such as vomiting, are general enough that an immediate diagnosis cannot be made.
The best indication of an internal poisoning is probably the presence of an open container of drugs or toxic household chemicals.
Consult the label for the first aid instructions specific to this poison.
The term “insect” is used by entomologists in a formal sense for this group of insects.
This term derives from an ancient familiarity with bed bugs, which are very suitable insects to parasitize humans.
Redusections, like bed bugs, are nestbockers, i.e. adapted to live in the nest or the habitat of their host.
In the United States, there are about 400 000 known cases of multiple sclerosis (MS), making it the main neurological disease in young and middle-aged adults.
Multiple sclerosis is a disease that affects the central nervous system, composed of the brain, spinal cord and optic nerve.
Research has shown that women are twice as likely to have multiple sclerosis as men.
A couple may very well feel that it is not in their interest, nor in that of the child, to take care of the education of a baby.
These couples can choose to make an adoption project for their baby.
As part of an adoption, biological parents end their parental rights so that another couple can raise the child.
The main objective of science is to understand how the world works through the scientific method. This method makes it most of the scientific research.
It is, however, not the only one, experimentation, and an experiment is a test that serves to eliminate one or more of the possible hypotheses, ask questions and make observations, but which also guides scientific research.
Naturalists and philosophers have focused on ancient texts, and in particular on the Bible in Latin.
Aristotle's views on all scientific issues, including psychology, were accepted.
With the decline of knowledge of Greek, the West found itself cut off from its Greek philosophical and scientific roots.
Many rhythms observed in physiology and behavior often depend largely on the presence of endogenous cycles and their production through biological clocks.
Periodic rhythms, which are not simply responses to external periodic stimuli, have been documented for most living beings, including bacteria, fungi, plants and animals.
Biological clocks are self-sufficient oscillators that will continue an operating cycle for a certain period of time, even in the absence of external stimulation.
The Hershey and Chase experiment was one of the main indications that DNA was a genetic material.
Hershey and Chase used phages, or viruses, to implant their own DNA into bacteria.
They conducted two experiments to mark either phage DNA with radioactive phosphorus or phage protein with radioactive sulfur.
Mutations can have many different effects depending on the type of mutation, the importance of the affected part of the genetic material, and if the affected cells are germinal cells.
Only germ cell mutations can be transmitted to children, while other mutations can lead to cell death or cancer.
Eco-tourism interests people who want to visit natural areas with the aim of enjoying the landscape, including fauna and flora.
Hunting, fishing, photography, bird watching, visiting parks and studying information about the ecosystem are examples of on-site activities.
For example, we can go see, photograph and discover the orangutans of Borneo.
Every morning, people leave small country towns by car to get to their workplaces and meet other people who are going to work in the countryside they’ve just left.
In this dynamic transportation shuttle, everyone is somehow connected to a transport system based on passenger cars and supports it.
Science now shows that this massive carbon-based economy has dislodged the biosphere from its state of stability that has supported human evolution for two million years.
Everyone is part of the company and uses transportation systems. Virtually everyone is complaining about transportation systems.
In developed countries, it is rare to hear so many complaints about water quality or the collapse of bridges.
Why do transportation systems generate such complaints, why do they fail daily? Are transport engineers simply incompetent? Or is something more fundamental?
The flow of traffic is the study of the movements of drivers and private vehicles between two points, and the interactions they exert between them.
Unfortunately, it is difficult to study the flow of traffic because the behavior of drivers cannot be predicted with one hundred percent certainty.
Luckily, conductors tend to act within a reasonably consistent range; thus, traffic flows tend to have reasonable consistency and can be represented mathematically approximately.
To better represent the flow of traffic, relationships have been established between the three main characteristics: (1) throughput, (2) density and (3) speed.
These relationships help with the planning, design, and operation of road facilities.
Insects were the first animals to fly. Their ability allowed them to more easily escape their enemies and more effectively find food and companions.
Most insects have the advantage of being able to fold their wings along the body.
This gives them a greater number of small places to hide from predators.
Today, the only insects that can't fold their wings are dragon flies and ephemerals.
A few thousand years ago, a man called Aristarch claimed that the solar system was moving around the Sun.
Some thought he was right, but many believed otherwise: that the solar system was moving around the Earth, including the Sun (and even other stars).
It seems reasonable, because the Earth doesn't feel like it's moving, does it?
The Azone River is the second longest and largest river on Earth. It carries more than eight times the volume of water from the second largest river.
The Amazon is also the widest river on the planet, sometimes reaching 10 km wide.
No less than 20% of the water flowing from the planet's rivers into the oceans comes from the Amazon.
The main arm of the Amazon River is 6,387 km (3 980 miles). It collects water from the thousands of small rivers.
Although the construction of stone pyramids continued until the end of the Old Kingdom, the pyramids of Giza were never surpassed by their size and the technical excellence of their construction.
The ancient Egyptians of the New Kingdom were amazed by the monuments of their predecessors, who were then much over a thousand years old.
The Vatican has about 800 inhabitants. It is the smallest independent country in the world, and the least populated.
Vatican City drafts its official legislation and communications in Italian.
Italian is also the everyday language used by most people working in the state, while Latin is often used in religious ceremonies.
The citizens of Vatican City are all Catholics.
Since antiquity, peoples have been familiar with basic chemical elements like gold, silver and copper, as they are found in nature in raw form and are quite simple to mine using primitive tools.
Aristotle, a philosopher, theorized that everything consists of a mixture of one or more of the four elements. It was earth, water, air and fire.
Rather, it was the four states of matter (in the same order): solid, liquid, gaseous and plasma, although it has also emitted the theory according to which they turn into new substances to form what we see.
Alloys are essentially a mixture of two or more metals. Do not forget that the periodic table comprises many elements.
Elements such as calcium and potassium are considered metals. Of course, there are also metals such as silver and gold.
There are also alloys that include a small amount of non-metallic elements, such as carbon.
Everything in the universe is made of matter. All matter is made of tiny particles called atoms.
Atoms are so incredibly small that billions of them could enter into the period at the end of that phrase.
So the pencil was a good friend for a lot of people when it came out.
Unfortunately, the emergence of new modes of writing has relegated the pen to a more confidential status and rarer uses.
People now write messages on computer screens, without ever having to get close to a pencil-size.
One can only wonder what the keyboard will become when the next novelty arrives.
The fission bomb operates according to the following principle: it transforms energy by assembling a nucleus of a myriad of protons and neutrons.
It's like climbing a hill to a heavy cart, dividing the nucleus again, then releasing a little of that energy.
Some atoms have an unstable nucleus, so they tend to break with little or no pressure.
The surface of the moon consists of stones and dust. Its outer layer is called the crust.
The thickness of the crust is about 70 km on the visible face and 100 km on the far side.
It is finer at sea level and thicker under the continents.
There may be more maria on the nearby side because the crust is thinner. It was easier for lava to rise to the surface.
Content theories are centered on finding what makes people feel good or attracted.
These theories suggest that people have certain needs and/or desires that have been internalized as they mature into adulthood.
These theories examine what drives some people to want the things they do and what, in their environment, drives them to do or don’t do certain things.
Two popular content theories are Maslow’s hierarchy of needs theory and Herzberg’s theory of two factors.
Generally speaking, two behaviors can emerge when a manager becomes the leader of former colleagues. From one end of the spectrum being to try to remain "the good friend" (or the good girlfriend).
This type of manager struggles to make unpopular decisions, carry out disciplinary actions, carry out evaluations, entrust responsibilities, and hold others accountable.
At the other end of the spectrum, one turns into an unrecognizable individual who feels he/she needs to change everything that the group has created so far and do things his/her way.
After all, the chef is ultimately responsible for the team’s success and failure.
This behavior often leads to dissentions between the leaders and the rest of the team.
Virtual teams are subject to the same standards of excellence as conventional teams, but there are subtle differences.
Members of a virtual team often function as a point of contact for their immediate physical group.
They often have more autonomy than traditional team members because their teams can come together according to different time zones, which may not be understood by their local management.
The presence of a real "invisible team" (Larson and LaFasto, 1989, p109) is also a unique part of a virtual team.
The "invisible team" is the management team to which each of the members is accountable. It sets the standards for each of them.
Why does an organization want to establish the tedious process of creating a learning structure? One of the objectives related to the implementation of organizational learning concepts is innovation.
When all available resources are used effectively in an organization's functional departments, creativity and ingenuity can be fully expressed.
Therefore, the process of an organization working together to overcome an obstacle can lead to an innovative new process to meet the customer’s needs.
Before an organisation is innovative, its leadership must create a culture of innovation, shared knowledge and its own learning culture.
Angel (2006) presents the Continuum approach as a method used to help organizations achieve better results.
Neurobiological data provide physical evidence for a theoretical approach to the study of cognition, so they reduce the field of research and make it much more accurate.
The correlation between the pathology of the brain and behavior supports scientists in their research.
It has long been known that different types of damage, lesions, tumors and brain trauma affect the behavior and cause changes in certain mental functions.
The development of new technologies allows us to see and study cerebral structures and processes never seen before.
It gives us a lot of information and hardware to build simulation models that help us understand the processes in our mind.
Although Artificial Intelligence is often highly connoted science fiction, it represents a very important part of the computer science that studies the behavior, learning and intelligent adaptation of a machine.
The purpose of research in artificial intelligence is the automation by task machines requiring an intelligent approach.
Examples include control, planning and programming, the ability to respond to customer diagnostics and questions, as well as the recognition of writing, voice and face.
These practices led to separate disciplines, focused on providing solutions to the problems of daily life.
The AI system is now often used in the fields of economics, medicine, engineering and the military, as it has been implemented in several home computer and video game software.
Excursions are an integral part of any school class. Quite often, a teacher would like to take his students to places where a bus trip is not possible.
The solution is provided by technology in the form of virtual educational outings. Students can look at the ancient objects of a museum, visit an aquarium, or admire a superb work of art while sitting with their classroom.
Participating practically in an excursion is also a great way to reflect on a trip and share your experiences with future generations.
For example, students from the Bennet School in North Carolina design an annual website dedicated to their visit to the state's capital. Each year, the site is updated, but older versions are kept online to serve as an album.
Blogs can also help students improve their writing skills. Even though students often try the blog experience with neglected grammar and spelling, having an audience usually changes the situation.
As students are often the most critical audience, the author of the blog is starting to strive to improve his writing to avoid criticism.
In addition, blogging "forces students to get to know the world around them". The need to generate public interest encourages students to be intelligent and interesting (Toto, 2004).
Blogs are a tool that stimulates collaboration and encourages students to extend their learning well beyond the traditional school day.
"When good use of it, blogs" can allow students to strengthen their analytical skills and critical thinking: by actively reacting to online content, they can compare their opinion with that of others and expose their own points of view on very specific topics" (Oravec, 2002)."
Ottawa is Canada's charming bilingual capital and has a large number of art galleries and museums that showcase Canada's past and present.
You’ll find the Niagara Falls further south and in the north, the Muskoka region and its surroundings with pristine natural beauty.
All of these measures, and so many others, make Ontario what foreigners see as Canada's quintessence.
Large areas further north are quite sparsely populated and some are almost uninhabited in the wild.
For a comparison of the population that surprises more than one: There are more African-Americans living in the United States than Canadian citizens.
The islands of East Africa are located in the Indian Ocean off the East African coast.
Madagascar is by far the largest country, and even a continent on its own, when it comes to wildlife.
Most of the small islands are independent nations, or associated with France, and are known to be luxury seaside resorts.
The Arabs also brought the practice of Islam into the lands and this religion spread widely to the Comoros and Mayotte.
European influence and colonialism began in the 15th century, at a time when the Portuguese explorer Vasco de Gama discovered the Cape Route that connects Europe with India.
To the north, the region is bounded by the Sahel, and to the south and west by the Atlantic Ocean.
To women: It is recommended to any woman who travels to say that she is married, regardless of her marital status.
It is also advisable to wear an alliance (which should not seem too valuable.
Women must realize that certain cultural differences can lead to behaviors that they consider to be harassment, because they are commonly tracked, caught by the arm, etc.
Be firm in refusing men's advances, and don't be afraid to hold on (cultural differences or not, that doesn't mean it's acceptable!)
The modern city of Casablanca was founded by Berber sinners in the 10th century BCE, and was used by the Phoenicians, Romans and Merinides as a strategic port called Anfa.
The Portuguese, which after destroying it had rebuilt it and named Casa Branca, finally abandoned it in 1755 after an earthquake.
The Moroccan sultan rebuilt the city under the name of Daru l-Badya, and it was renamed Casablanca by the Spanish merchants who established trading counters there.
Casablanca is one of the less interesting places in all of Morocco for shopping.
Around the old medina, you can easily find places to buy traditional Moroccan products, such as tajines, pottery, leather goods, hookahs and trinkets of all kinds, but that's all for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the far east of the Democratic Republic of Congo, near Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by the lava of Nyiragongo Volcano which buried most of the city’s streets, especially the city center.
While Goma is reasonably safe, any visits outside Goma must be researched to understand the state of fighting that persists in North Kivu province.
The city is also the starting point for the ascent of Nyiragongo Volcano as well as mountain gorilla tracking treks among the cheapest in Africa.
You can use boda-boda (motorbike taxi) to get around Goma. The normal (local) price is about 500 Congolese francs for a small ride.
Combined with its relative inaccessibility, “Timbuktu” has become a metaphor for distant and exotic countries.
Timbuktu is a poor city, although its reputation makes it a tourist attraction, and it has an airport.
In 1990, it was addered to the list of endangered World Heritage sites, due to the threat of desert sands.
It was one of the main milestones of Henry Louis Gates' "Wonders of the African World" on the PBS.
The city contrasts sharply with the cities of the rest of the country, as it possesses a more Arab than African style.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in the north-east of South Africa and runs along the border from Mozambique to the east, from Zimbabwe to the north and the southern border is the Crocodile River.
The total area of the park is 19 500 km2, it is divided into 14 ecozones, each housing a specific fauna.
It is one of the best-known amusement parks in South Africa and considered the flagship of South African National Parks (SANParks).
As with all South African national parks, there are daily admission and conservation fees for the park.
It can be beneficial to get a Wild Card that provides access to some parks in South Africa, as well as all South African national parks.
Hong Kong Island gives its name to Hong Kong territory and is the main point of interest for many tourists.
The row of buildings that draw Hong Kong's horizon has been compared to a glittering bar graph, which is revealed by reflection in the waters of Victoria Harbour.
To see Hong Kong’s finest panoramas, leave the island and reach the Kowloon waterfront in front.
The vast majority of Hong Kong Island’s urban development is concentrated on the land of the North Shore.
This is the place that the British settlers have invested, so this seems to me to be a good starting point, if you are looking for evidence of the colonial past of this region.
Sundarbans are the world’s largest coastal mangrove belt, stretching 80 km (50 miles) from the coast to the Bangladeshi and Indian hinterlands.
Sundarbans have been declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The part of the forest located on Indian territory is referred to as Sundarbans National Park.
However, the forests are not composed only of mangrove marshes: they also contain some of the last remnants of the majestic jungles that once covered the Ganges plain.
Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km2, of which approximately one-third is covered with water/swamp areas.
Sundarbans has been a natural reserve since 1966, where you can find about 400 Royal Bengal tigers and 30 000 spotted deers.
The buses depart from the interdistrict bus station (located on the other shore) throughout the day, but most, especially those serving the east and Jakar/Bumthang, depart between 6:30 a.m. and 7:30 a.m.
Inter-district buses are often complete, so it is advisable to buy a ticket a few days in advance.
Most districts are served by small Japanese coasters, which are comfortable and sturdy.
Collective taxis are a fast and comfortable way to get to nearby places, such as Paro (150 Nude) and Punakha (200 Nude).
The bridge over the Oyapock is a haubanch bridge. It overlooks the Oyapock River to connect the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges-de-l'Oyapock in Guyana.
The two towers rise to a height of 83 meters; it is 378 meters long and has two lanes 3.50 m wide.
Vertical clearance under the deck is 15 meters. The structure was completed in August 2011, but it was not opened to traffic until March 2017.
The bridge is expected to be fully operational in September 2017, when the Brazilian customs checkpoints should be completed.
The Guaraní were the most important indigenous group inhabiting what is now eastern Paraguay, living as semi-nomadic hunters and also practicing subsistence farming.
Other indigenous tribes that inhabited Chaco, such as Guaycurú or Payaguá, survived mainly through fishing, hunting, or gathering.
In the 16th century, Paraguay, formerly dubbed “the giant province of Indies”, was born out of the encounter between Spanish conquerors and indigenous populations.
The Spaniards inaugurated the period of colonization, which lasted three centuries.
Since Asunción was founded in 1537, Paraguay has managed to retain much of its indigenous character and identity.
Argentina is known to have one of the best teams and some of the best polo players in the world.
The biggest tournament of the year takes place in December on the polo courts of Las Cañitas.
In addition, tournaments and smaller matches are sometimes held here at other times of the year.
For information on tournaments and places to buy tickets for polo matches, check out the Asociacion Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the pound of the Falkland Islands (FKP), the value of which is equivalent to a pound sterling (GBP).
The money can be exchanged at the only island bank located in Stanley, in front of the FIC West store.
Pound sterling is a commonly accepted currency all over the islands, while in Stanley, credit cards and US dollars are also often accepted.
On the surrounding islands, credit cards will probably not be accepted, even though British and U.S. currencies may be accepted. Talk about them in advance with the owners in order to agree on a payment method.
It’s almost impossible to exchange Falkland money outside the islands, so change it before you leave the islands.
Montevideo being located in the south of Ecuador, winter in the northern hemisphere corresponds to the summer there and vice versa.
Montevideo is located in the subtropical zone and summer temperatures exceeding 30 °C are common.
Winter can be deceptively cold: Temperatures rarely drop below zero, but wind and humidity combine to give the impression that it’s colder than what the thermometer says.
There are really no “rainy” and “dry” seasons: rainfall is almost constant throughout the year.
Although many animals in the park are accustomed to seeing humans, they are nevertheless wildlife; please do not feed or disturb them.
According to the park authorities, you have to stand at least 100 meters from bears and wolves and 25 meters from all other wild animals!
As docile as they may seem, buffalo, momentum, moose, bear and almost all large animals can attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors are injured because they have not kept a sufficient distance. These animals are large, wild and potentially dangerous, so give them space.
In addiction, know that odors attract bears and other wild animals, so avoid transporting or cooking fragrant food and keep the camp clean.
Apia is the capital of Samoa. The city is located on the island of Upolu and has a little less than 40 000 inhabitants.
Apia was founded in the 1850s and has been the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
The port was the site of an infamous naval impasse in 1889, when seven ships from Germany, the United States and Great Britain refused to leave the port.
All ships were sank, with the exception of a British cruiser. Nearly 200 Americans and Germans lost their lives.
Supreme leader Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III was assassinated at a peaceful rally held in the city, during the independence struggle organized by the Mau movement.
There are many beaches, as Auckland is straddling on two ports. The most popular are found in three areas.
The beaches of the North Shore (in the district of North Harbour) are located on the Pacific Ocean and extend from Long Bay in the north to Devonport in the south.
Almost all the beaches are sandy and make it possible to swim safely, and most are shaded by pohutukawa.
Tamaki Drive beaches can be found on Waitemata Harbour, in the chic suburbs of Mission Bay and Saint Heliers in central Auckland.
These beaches, sometimes very frequented by families, have many shops along the coast. Swimming is safe.
The main local beer is the “Number One”, a fairly ordinary, but tasty and refreshing beer. There is also another local beer, the “Manta”.
There is a lot of French wines to taste, but New Zealand and Australian wines may travel better.
You can drink local tap water safely, but you can easily find bottled water if you have fears about it.
For Australians, the idea of a "cream coffee" is foreign. A short black is an "espresso", cappuccino is filled with cream (no foam) and tea is served without milk.
Hot chocolate complies with Belgian standards. Fruit juices are expensive but excellent.
Many trips to the reef are made throughout the year, and injuries due to one of these causes on the reef are rare.
Nevertheless, follow the advice of the authorities, obey all the signs and pay particular attention to the safety warnings.
From October to April, north of 1770, box jellyfish arrive near beaches and river estuaries. They can also sometimes be sighted outside this period.
Sharks exist, but they rarely attack humans. Most of them are afraid of humans and would deviate from them.
Salted water crocodiles do not actively live in the ocean, their main habitat is found in river estuaries north of Rockhampton.
The reservation in advance gives the traveler the peace of mind to know that he will have a place to sleep once he arrives at his destination.
Travel agencies often have agreements with specific hotels, but you can also book through travel agencies other types of accommodation, such as camping areas.
Travel agencies typically offer packages that include breakfast, transportation service from/to the airport, or even packages that combine flight and accommodation.
They can also retain the reservation for you if you need time to think about the offer or get some other necessary documents for your destination (e.g. a visa).
However, any modification or request must be addressed to the travel agency first and not directly to the hotel.
At some music festivals, the vast majority of participants decide to camp on site, and most consider this an integral part of the experience.
If you want to participate closely in the musical event, you will have to arrive early to find a well-placed camping area.
Don’t forget that even if the musical shows are completed on the main stages, other musical performances can continue until late in the night at other festival venues.
Some festivals have special camping areas for families with young children.
If you pass through the north of the Baltic in winter, be sure to check the location of the hut, as crossing the ice can cause unbearable noises at the most exposed huts.
Cruises in St. Petersburg include time spent in the city. Cruise passengers are exempt from the visa requirement (see conditions).
Casinos usually make many efforts to maximize the time and money spent by customers. There are rarely windows and clocks, and exits can be difficult to spot.
They usually offer special offers of dishes, beverages and entertainment in order to maintain the good mood of the guests and keep them on site.
On some sites, it is the locals who offer alcoholic beverages for free. Nevertheless, intoxication alters the judgment, and all good players know that it is crucial to remain sober.
Anyone driving at high latitudes or crossing mountain passes should consider the possibility of snow, ice or freezing temperatures.
On icy and snowy roads, the friction is low and you can’t drive like you’re on naked asphalt.
During blizzards it can fall in a very short time enough snow to block you.
Visibility can also be limited by snowfall or daunting or by condensation or ice on vehicle windows.
On the other hand, the presence of ice and snow is normal in many countries, and traffic is virtually uninterrupted throughout the year.
Safaris are perhaps the greatest tourist attraction in Africa and the most striking moment for many visitors.
In everyday language, the term "safari" refers to an excursion on African land, and in particular in the savannah, with the aim of witnessing the striking spectacle of wild animals in their environment.
Some animals, such as elephants and giraffes, tend to get up close to cars and the standard equipment makes it possible to observe them well.
Lions, cheetahs and leopards are sometimes shy and you’ll see them better with twins.
A safari on foot (also known as “bush walking”, “pedestrian safari” or “jogging”) consists of hiking, either for a few hours or for several days.
Paralympics will be held from August 24 to September 5, 2021. Some events will be held in other locations across Japan.
Tokyo will be the only Asian city to have hosted two Summer Olympic Games, having hosted the Games in 1964.
If you have booked your flights and accommodation in 2020 before the announcement of the postponement, you may be confronted with a delicate situation.
Cancellation policies vary, but from the end of March, most coronavirus-based cancellation policies do not extend until July 2020, when the Olympics were scheduled.
It is expected that most event tickets will cost between 2 500 and 130 000 yen, with typical banknotes costing about 7 000 yen.
By ironing wet clothes, you can dry them more easily. Many hotels have an iron and an ironing board that can be loaned, even if there is none in the rooms.
If you don't have an iron or don't feel like wearing ironed socks, you can try to use a hair dryer, if you find one.
Do not allow the fabric to overheat (which can cause shrinkage or in extreme cases, burn it).
There are different methods of water purification, some of which are more effective against specific threats.
In some localities, it is enough to boil the water for one minute; in others, it must be done for several minutes.
Filter efficiency varies, and if you’re concerned, you should consider buying your water in a sealed bottle from a reputable company.
Travellers may encounter pests they don’t know in their region of origin.
Pests can contaminate food, cause irritation or, in the most severe cases, cause allergic reactions, spread venom, or pass infections.
Infectious diseases themselves, or dangerous animals that can injure or kill people by force, are generally not considered parasites.
In some localities, duty-free shops present an opportunity to purchase tax and excise duty exempt goods.
Travellers to countries with high taxes can sometimes make considerable savings, especially on products such as alcoholic beverages or tobacco.
The section between Point Marion and Fairmont presents the most difficult driving conditions on the Buffalo-Pittsburgh Highway, frequently passing through isolated terrain from the hinterland.
If you’re not used to driving on country roads, keep an open mind: steep slopes, narrow lanes and tight curves predominate.
The speed limits displayed are significantly lower than in the previous and subsequent sections – usually from 35 to 40 mph (56-64 km/h) – and their strict compliance is even greater than before.
On the other hand, the mobile service is curiously much stronger here than on many other parts of the road, such as the Pennsylvania Wilds.
German pastries are rather good, and in Bavaria they are very rich and varied, similar to those of their southern neighbour, Austria.
Fruit pastries are usual: apples are cooked and cooked in pastry all year round, while cherries and plums point the tip of their nose in summer.
Many German baked goods also contain almonds, hazelnuts and other nuts. Popular cakes often pair particularly well with a cup of tight coffee.
If you want small but hearty pastries, try Berlin balls, also called Berliner, Pfannkuchen or Krapfen according to the regions.
A curry is a dish made from herbs and spices, accompanied by meat or vegetables.
A curry dish can be "solid" or "liquid", depending on the amount of sauce it contains.
In the inland regions of northern India and Pakistan, yogurt is commonly used in curries; in southern India and some other coastal regions of the subcontinent, coconut milk is commonly used.
Indonesia with 17 000 islands, Indonesian cuisine is a generic term that covers a wide variety of regional cuisines present throughout the country.
However, when this term is used without any other qualifier, it tends to designate food from the central and eastern parts of the main island of Java.
Now, widely available throughout the archipelago, Javanese cuisine offers a range of dishes simply seasoned, the predominant flavors that the Javanese prefer being peanuts, peppers, sugar (especially Javanese coconut sugar) and various aromatic spices.
Stirrups are supports for the feet of the rider, hanging on either side of the saddle.
They offer greater stability to the user, but they can pose a security problem: indeed, the user's feet can get stuck there.
If a rider is thrown from a horse, but has one foot caught in the stirrup, it could be dragged if the horse flees. To minimize this risk, a number of safety measures can be taken.
First, most riders wear riding boots with a heel and sole smooth and fairly narrow.
Then, some stools, especially the English, have safety bars that allow a streaker to fall from the saddle if it is pulled backwards by a falling rider.
Cochamó Valley – The destination of excellence for climbing in Chile, known as South America’s Yosemite, with many different large granite walls and cliffs.
From the top of the peaks, the panoramas of the mountains are impregnable. Mountaineers from all over the world are constantly discovering new passable paths on the slopes, where the possibilities are unlimited.
Descending winter sports, including skiing and snowboarding, are popular sports that consist of sliding to run down a snowy slope with skis or a snowboard attached to the feet.
Skiing is a major travel activity that has many aficionados, sometimes called “ski bums”, which organize their entire holidays according to skiing in specific places.
The idea of skiing is very old: cave paintings showing skiers date for some 5 000 years BC!
The practice of alpine skiing as a sport dates back to at least the seventeenth century; the first recreational ski club was founded in 1861 in Australia by Norwegians.
Ski hiking : this activity is also called Nordic skiing, cross-country skiing or ski touring.
It is similar to alpine-type touring skiing, as well as mountaineering, without generally incorporating these activities, which are practiced on rough terrain and which require the use of skis and boots that are much more rigid.
Think of the ski route as a similar hiking route.
In good conditions, you’ll be able to travel distances a little larger than on foot – but you’ll very rarely reach cross-country ski speeds without a heavy backpack on groomed slopes.
Europe is a relatively small continent, but with many independent countries. Under traditional circumstances, traveling across several countries therefore implies having to make several visa applications and undergo several passport checks.
The Schengen area, however, works a bit like a single country in this regard.
As long as you stay in this area, you can usually cross the borders without passing through passport checkpoints.
Likewise, by having a Schengen visa, you do not need to apply separately for a visa for each of the member countries of the space, which saves time, money and administrative formalities.
There is no universal definition to determine whether a manufactured object is an antiquity. Some tax organizations define property of more than 100 years as antiquities.
The criteria vary according to the location; in some parts of the world, such as in North America, the age limit may be lower than that in Europe.
Handmade products can be considered antiques, even if they are less old than similar industrial products.
Reindeer farming is an important means of subsistence among the Sámi, and the cultivation that surrounds this trade is of great importance to many other professions.
Although, according to tradition, not all Samis were involved in large-scale reindeering, they lived on fishing, hunting and other similar activities, while using reindeers primarily as draft animals.
Nowadays, many Sámi practice modern trades. Tourism is an important source of income in Lapland, the Sámi region.
Although widely used, especially among non-Romas, the term “Gypsy” is often considered derogatory because of its association with negative stereotypes and a distorted image of the Roma people.
If the country to which you are travelling is the subject of a travel warning issued by your government, your foreign health insurance or travel cancellation insurance may be affected.
You can also use the advice of other governments other than your own, but their advice is tailored to their citizens.
As an example, U.S. citizens in the Middle East may face situations that are different from those of Europeans or Arabs.
Opinions are just a brief summary of a country's political situation.
The opinions presented are often superficial, general and too simplified compared to the more detailed information available elsewhere.
Severe weather is the generic term for any dangerous meteorological phenomenon likely to cause damage, serious social upheaval or loss of life.
Very unfavourable weather conditions can occur anywhere in the world, and they can be of many kinds, depending on the geographical, topographical and atmospheric characteristics.
Severe winds, hail, excessive rainfall and fires are forms and effects of severe weather, as are thunderstorms, tornadoes, sea trombles and cyclones.
Extreme, regional and seasonal weather events include cold storms, snowstorms with high winds, and sand or dust storms.
Travellers are strongly advised to learn about the risks of bad weather that may affect their area of destination, knowing that this can have an impact on the itineraries they are considering.
Anyone planning to travel to a country that could be considered a war zone should receive vocational training.
An internet search of “Hostile Environment Courses” will likely provide the address of a local company.
A course will normally cover all the issues discussed here in a much more detailed way, usually with hands-on experience.
Training normally lasts 2 to 5 days and includes role-playing games, a lot of first aid, and sometimes training to handle weapons.
Books and magazines dealing wild survival are common, but there are few publications dealing with war zones.
Travellers planning a sex-changing operation abroad must ensure that they have valid documents in their possession for their return trip.
The willingness of governments to issue passports whose gender is not indicated (X) or documents updated to match the desired name and sex varies.
The willingness of foreign governments to honour these documents is equally variable.
Excavations at security checkpoints also became much more intrusive in the post-September 11, 2001 era.
Transsexuals in the preoperative phase should not expect to pass the scanners while preserving their privacy and dignity.
Tearing currents are the currents that return from waves that break on the beach, often on a pitfall or equivalent.
Due to the underwater topology, the return flow is concentrated on a few deeper sections, and a rapid current towards the deep waters can form there.
In most cases, deaths are due to exhaustion that results from an attempt to swim against the current, which may be impossible.
As soon as you get out of power, getting back to swimming isn't more difficult than usual.
Try to aim for a place you won’t be taken again. It’s also possible that depending on your skills and whether you’ve been spotted or not, you may want to wait for help.
Reintegration shock occurs earlier than culture shock (the honeymoon phase is less longer), lasts longer and can be more severe.
Travelers who have struggled to adapt to the new culture sometimes find it very difficult to rehabilitate their original culture.
When you return home after living abroad, you have already adapted to the new culture and lost certain habits of your original culture.
At first, when you went abroad, people were probably patient and understanding, knowing that travelers in a new country have to adapt.
People may not anticipate that patience and understanding are also necessary for travelers and travelers returning home.
The sound and light show to the pyramid is one of the region’s most interesting attractions for children.
You can see the pyramids in the darkness and you can see them silently before the show begins.
Usually, we always hear the noise of tourists and sellers. The history of sound and light is like a history book.
The Sphinx is placed as the backdrop and narrator of a long history.
Scenes are displayed on the pyramids and the different pyramids are illuminated.
The Southern Shetland Islands, discovered in 1819, are claimed by several nations and have the largest number of research stations and sixteen of them are active in 2020.
The archipelago is 120 km north of the peninsula. The island of King George, with the village of Villa Las Estrellas, is the largest.
Other examples: Livingston Island and Deception Island, where the flooded caldera of a still active volcano offers a spectacular natural harbor.
Ellsworth Land is the southern region of the peninsula, bounded by the Bellingshausen Sea.
The mountains of the peninsula blend into the plateau, then reappear to form Ellsworth's 360-kilometer-long mountain range, crossed by the Minnesota Glacier.
The northern part, or Sentinel range, has the highest mountains in Antarctica, the Vinson massif, which peaks at 4892 m.
In remote locations, without a cell phone network, you may have no choice but to use satellite telephony.
A satellite phone usually does not replace a mobile phone, because you have to be outside and have an unobstructed view of the satellite to make a call.
It is a service often used by navigation, including pleasure craft, as well as shipments that have the need for remote data transmission and telephony.
Your local telephone operator should be able to tell you more about connecting to this service.
Those planning a sabbatical year have the opportunity to learn while traveling, and more and more of them choose this possibility.
This formula is particularly appreciated by young people who leave school, because it allows them to take a year off before university, without compromising their studies.
Often, having taken sabbatical year courses abroad can actually give you a better chance of higher education in your country.
As a general rule, you have to pay a registration fee to follow these educational programs.
Finland is a destination of choice for boating. The “Land of a Thousand Lakes” also has thousands of islands, in lakes and coastal archipelagos.
You don't necessarily need a yacht in the archipelagos and on the lakes.
Although coastal archipelagos and larger lakes are actually large enough for a yacht, smaller boats or even a kayak offer a different experience.
Boating is a national pastime in Finland, at the rate of one boat for seven or eight people.
Norway, Sweden and New Zealand are in the same situation, but apart from that, it is a unique case (in the Netherlands, for example, the figure is from one to forty).
Most cruises on the Baltic are characterized by an extended stay in St. Petersburg, Russia.
This means that you can visit the historic city for a few whole days while returning to sleep on the boat at night.
If you only go ashore with cruising excursions, you won’t need a separate visa (from 2009).
The city of Berlin, Germany, is mentioned in some brochures for cruises. As you can see by consulting the map above, Berlin is not at all located near the seaside and no tour of this city is included in the price of the cruise.
Flying can be a scary experience for people of all ages and backgrounds, especially if they have never flown before or experienced a traumatic event.
We must not be ashamed of it: it is no different from the personal fears and fears that many people experience towards other things.
For some, understanding something about how airplanes work and what happens during a flight can help overcome a fear that is based on the unknown or not having control.
Transport companies are well compensated for fast deliveries. Very often, as part of an urgent repair, the issue of deadlines is crucial for the documents, goods or spare parts related to them.
On some roads, large companies have their own planes, but on other roads and for small businesses there has been a problem.
If they had sent goods by air freight on some routes, it would have taken days to pass the unloading and customs.
The only way to get it faster was to send it as checked baggage. Air regulations will not allow them to send luggage without a passenger, and that's where you intervene.
The most obvious way to travel in first class or business class is to pay a large amount of money for this privilege (or, even better, to ask your company to do it for you).
However, this is not cheap: as a general rule, you can expect to pay up to four times the normal economic rate for the business class, and eleven times for the first class!
In general, there is no need to seek discounts for seats in business class or first-class on direct flights from A to B.
Airlines are well aware that they can count on a certain type of travelers who are willing to pay very dearly the privileges of speed and comfort, and they establish their tariffs knowingly.
The capital of Moldova is Chişinău. The local language is Romanian, but Russian is widespread.
Moldova is a multi-ethnic republic suffering from ethnic conflicts.
In 1994, this conflict led to the creation of the self-proclaimed Republic of Transnistria in Eastern Moldova, with its own government and currency, but recognized by any United Nation member country.
Economic ties have been restored between these two sides of Moldova despite the failure of political negotiations.
The main religion in Moldova is Orthodox Christianity.
With a population of 3.7 million, Izmir is Turkey’s third-largest city, the second-largest port after Istanbul, and an excellent connecting platform.
What was the ancient city of Smyrna is today a modern shopping center, developed and alive, installed around a huge bay, surrounded by mountains.
The wide boulevards, glazed buildings and modern shopping centers are dotted with traditional red tile roofs, the 18th-century market, and old mosques and churches, although the city has an atmosphere reminiscent of Mediterranean Europe more than traditional Turkey.
Haldarsvík village offers views of the nearby island of Eysturoy and is home to an unusual octagonal church.
In the cemetery there are interesting marble sculptures depicting doves on some tombs.
It’s worth walking for half an hour in this fascinating village.
The romantic and fascinating city of Sintra, located to the north, is easily accessible and has been made famous among foreigners by the vivid account of its splendor that Lord Byron made of it.
Scotturb’s 403 bus travels regularly to Sintra, with a stop in Cabo da Roca.
On your way north, don’t forget to visit the beautiful Shrine of Our Lady of Fatima (temple), a place known worldwide for Mary’s apparitions.
Don’t forget that you’re essentially visiting a mass grave, as well as a site that has an almost incalculable meaning for a significant part of the world’s population.
There are still many alive women and men who have survived their sojourn here, and many others who have seen their loved ones murdered or killed by forced labour, Jewish or non-Jewish indifferently.
Please treat the site with all the dignity, solemnity and respect it deserves. Don’t make jokes about the Holocaust or the Nazis.
Don’t disfigure the site by scoring or scratching graffiti on the structures.
The official languages of Barcelona are Catalan and Spanish. About half of the locals prefer to speak Catalan, the vast majority understand it, and virtually everyone is able to speak Spanish.
However, most of the signs are only in Catalan, because the law establishes that it is the first official language.
However, Spaniard is also widely used in public transport and other facilities.
Regular announcements in the subway are made only in Catalan, but unforeseen disturbances are announced by an automated system in many languages, including Spanish, English, French, Arabic and Japanese.
Parisians have the reputation of being self-centered, coarse and arrogant.
Although this stereotype is often inaccurate, the best way to get along in Paris is to behave at best, to act as a well-behaved. This will greatly facilitate your movements.
The abrupt exteriors of the Parisians evaporate quickly if you show a minimum of courtesy.
Plitvice Lakes National Park is densely wooded, mainly beeches, spruces and fir trees, and has a mixture of alpine and Mediterranean vegetation.
The country has a wide variety of plant communities due to the diversity of its microclimates, soils and altitude levels.
The area is also home to a very wide variety of animal species and birds.
Rare animals such as European brown bears, wolf, eagle, owl, lynx, wild cat and large tetras can be seen, as well as many other common species.
When visiting a monastery, women should wear skirts covering their knees and have shoulders also covered.
Most monasteries provide stoles to women who arrive without anything, but if you bring your own, and in addition it has bright colors, you will be entitled to a smile from the monk or nun at the entrance.
Just as men have to wear trousers covering their knees.
You can also borrow this garment from the stock at the entrance, but since it is not washed after each use, you may not feel comfortable wearing these skirts.
The Mallorcan cuisine, such as that of similar areas of the Mediterranean, is based on bread, vegetables and meat (especially pork), and uses olive oil.
A simple and popular dinner, especially in the summer, is Pa amb Oli: bread with olive oil, tomato and all available condiments, such as cheese, tuna, etc.
All the names, followed by the word Sie which means you, always begin with a capital letter, even in the middle of a sentence.
This is a great way to distinguish between some verbs and objects.
It can also be said that it facilitates reading, although writing is somewhat complicated by the need to know whether a verb or an adjective is used in a substantivized form.
Pronuncation is relatively easy in Italian, because most of the words are pronounced exactly as they are written
The main letters to monitor are c and g, because their pronunciation varies according to the following vowel.
Please also make sure to pronounce “r” and “rr” in a distinct way: “caro” means “dearly”, while “carro” means “trolley”.
Persan has a relatively easy gram and most of the time regular.
Therefore, reading this introduction to grammar would help you learn a lot about Persian grammar and better understand sentences.
It goes without saying that if you are familiar with a Romance language, it will be easier for you to learn Portuguese.
However, those who speak a little Spanish might hastily conclude that the Portuguese is close enough that it is not necessary to study it separately.
Premodern observatories are generally obsolete today, and remain in place as museums or education sites.
As light pollution in their time wasn't as worrying as it is today, they're usually located in cities or on campuses that are easier to access than those built in modern times.
Most modern research telescopes are huge infrastructures placed in remote areas where atmospheric conditions are favorable.
The contemplation of cherry blossoms, known as hanami, has been part of Japanese culture since the 8th century.
The concept came from China where plum blossoms were the favorite flower.
In Japan, the first festivals of cherry blossoms were organized by the emperor only for himself and the other members of the nearby aristocracy around the Imperial Court.
Plants have their most beautiful appearance when found in a natural environment, so resist the temptation to remove even “only one”.
If you visit a strictly landscaped garden, the collection of “speciples” will also make you deport without discussion.
Singapore is generally considered an extremely safe place, where it’s very easy to get around, and where you’ll be able to buy almost everything you need once you’re there.
But, located in the "high tropics" just a few degrees north of the equator, you will have to adapt to the heat (always) and a strong radiation from the sun (when the sky is clear, which is rarer).
